What to look for
A good answer:

describes magnitude rather than using a significance label;
distinguishes uncertainty in the mean from between-setting variation;
does not turn an observational moderator association into a causal claim;
does not claim that funnel diagnostics prove presence or absence of publication bias;
states the relevant evidence context rather than assuming universal applicability.

Learning objectives
By the end of this module, you should be able to:

translate model output into substantive language;
distinguish mean effects from expected variation across settings;
avoid significance-based conclusions;
separate evidence about effects from assumptions about transportability;
integrate risk of bias, reporting bias and sensitivity analyses;
write a concise, calibrated results paragraph for a decision-maker.

Do not turn the result into a vote
Avoid:

“The intervention works because the pooled effect was statistically significant.”

Prefer to describe:

magnitude;
uncertainty;
heterogeneity and prediction;
evidence credibility;
contexts represented by the evidence.


The question is not “did p cross 0.05?” but “what does the evidence support?”


This deliberately echoes Module 2 and the sensitivity module. Statistical significance is neither effect magnitude nor practical importance, and it does not establish consistency or causal validity.

Every meta-analysis contains choices
Examples include:

eligibility decisions;
effect-size definitions;
handling of dependence;
heterogeneity estimators;
moderator specification;
treatment of high-risk-of-bias studies;
assumptions about missing correlations.


Would another defensible choice change what we tell the decision-maker?


Frame sensitivity analysis as part of the scientific argument. It is not a ritual performed after the primary model and it is not restricted to leave-one-out diagnostics.

Learning objectives
By the end of this module, you should be able to:

distinguish sensitivity analysis from influence diagnostics;
identify assumptions worth varying;
use leave-one-out and influence diagnostics appropriately;
compare defensible model specifications;
incorporate risk of bias into sensitivity analyses;
report robustness without reducing it to significance/no significance.

Leave-one-out: what are we looking for?
For each omitted study/effect, examine changes in:

pooled magnitude;
uncertainty;
prediction interval;
heterogeneity;
substantive interpretation.


Do not ask only whether \(p\) crosses 0.05.


A result moving from p=.04 to p=.06 with essentially the same estimate is not a dramatic lack of robustness. Conversely, a conclusion can remain statistically significant while changing enough in magnitude to matter for decisions.

Risk of bias belongs in robustness thinking
Possible approaches include:

primary analysis using all eligible evidence, with RoB clearly characterised;
sensitivity analysis excluding studies with specified high-risk features;
stratification/meta-regression where justified and adequately powered;
interpretation that gives greater weight to more credible evidence.


A numerical “quality score” is not a substitute for domain-level critical appraisal.


Emphasise prespecification. Excluding inconvenient studies after seeing their results is not a credible sensitivity analysis. Risk-of-bias domains should reflect mechanisms by which estimates could be biased.

What if nothing changes?
Robustness increases confidence with respect to the assumptions tested.
It does not prove absence of:

publication bias;
unmeasured confounding;
outcome reporting bias;
measurement error;
poor external validity.


Robust to tested alternatives ≠ universally trustworthy.


Avoid overclaiming from robustness. Sensitivity analyses are conditional checks, not validation of the whole evidence base.

One paper does not always mean one effect
A study may report several:

outcomes;
treatment levels;
time points;
sites;
comparisons sharing the same control;
effect sizes calculated from the same participants or plots.


More rows ≠ more independent information.


Ask participants how often they have extracted more than one effect from a paper. In agri-food evidence this is routine: several soil depths, crops, treatments, outcomes or follow-up times. The problem is not multiple outcomes themselves; it is pretending that their sampling errors and underlying effects are unrelated.

Learning objectives
By the end of this module, you should be able to:

recognise common forms of dependence in meta-analysis;
explain why naive independence can distort precision and weighting;
distinguish study-level and effect-level variation;
fit a simple multilevel model with rma.mv();
identify when a sampling variance–covariance matrix may be needed;
explain what a multilevel model does not solve.

What goes wrong if we ignore it?
Treating correlated effects as independent can:

give studies with many outcomes disproportionate weight;
underestimate uncertainty;
produce confidence intervals that are too narrow;
obscure variation within versus between studies;
make moderator tests look more informative than they are.


The pooled estimate itself may also shift when prolific studies differ systematically from the rest.


Avoid saying that dependence always causes the same bias direction. The most predictable problem is incorrect uncertainty and weighting. The point estimate can also change because studies contributing many effects get more influence.

What if correlations are unknown?
Possible strategies include:

obtain enough primary information to calculate covariance;
make and vary a plausible correlation assumption;
aggregate effects when scientifically justified;
use cluster-robust inference where appropriate;
test whether conclusions depend on the chosen strategy.


“We do not know the correlation” is a reason for sensitivity analysis — not a reason to assume zero silently.


Keep this as a map rather than a full robust variance estimation lesson. The course can point learners towards advanced resources. The key habit is to make the dependence assumption visible.

What the model cannot fix
A multilevel model does not automatically fix:

biased primary studies;
incompatible outcomes;
selective outcome reporting;
badly measured moderators;
confounding;
extrapolation beyond represented settings.


A more complex model cannot rescue an incoherent synthesis question.


Connect back to critical appraisal and estimands. Statistical sophistication is not a substitute for design judgement.

Learning objectives
By the end of this module, you should be able to:

explain why simple averaging is usually inappropriate;
distinguish common-effect and random-effects assumptions;
fit a random-effects model using metafor;
read a forest plot;
distinguish confidence and prediction intervals;
translate a pooled lnRR into an interpretable result.


Keep returning to the estimand. A model is useful only insofar as its assumptions correspond to the scientific quantity we want to estimate.

Forest plot: read the evidence, not just the diamond
A forest plot shows:

each effect estimate;
uncertainty around each estimate;
relative statistical precision;
the pooled estimate;
sometimes a prediction interval.


Look at the distribution before looking at the summary.


Ask where participants’ eyes go first. Many people jump to the pooled diamond or count intervals crossing the null. Encourage them to inspect magnitude, precision and variation across studies first.

Don’t vote count the forest plot

Avoid: “8 of 12 studies found a significant positive effect.”

That discards:

magnitude;
precision;
uncertainty;
between-study variation.


Synthesis uses the estimates and their uncertainty directly.


Distinguish narrative description from vote counting. It is fine to describe the pattern and direction of effects. Tallying statistical significance is not an appropriate synthesis method.

Learning objectives
By the end of this module, you should be able to:

explain what a meta-analysis does — and does not do;
translate an agri-food question into a clear estimand;
distinguish the target question from the available data;
identify when studies are sufficiently compatible to combine;
recognise why model choice comes after defining the question.


Open by asking participants what they hope a meta-analysis gives them. Common answers are “an overall effect”, “more power”, or “a definitive answer”. Keep these answers visible if possible. Return to them at the end.
The central message of the module is that meta-analysis is not primarily a software procedure. It is a way of estimating a clearly defined quantity from multiple studies.

What is meta-analysis?

Meta-analysis is the statistical synthesis of results from multiple studies addressing a sufficiently similar question.

It can help us estimate:

the average effect;
uncertainty around that average;
variation in effects among contexts;
whether observed differences can be explained by study characteristics.


Emphasise “sufficiently similar question”. Statistical compatibility does not establish conceptual compatibility. We can always put numbers into a model; that does not mean the resulting pooled estimate answers a useful question.

What evidence belongs together?
Two studies can both mention “soil carbon” and still estimate different things.
Consider differences in:

soil carbon measure and depth;
intervention definition;
comparator;
follow-up duration;
experimental design;
climate and soil system;
unit of analysis.

Pooling is a scientific judgement before it is a statistical operation.

Ask: “Would you combine soil organic carbon measured at 0–10 cm after one year with total carbon at 0–30 cm after 20 years?” There is no automatic yes/no answer. The point is that compatibility must be justified.

Variation is not necessarily noise
Agri-food interventions often operate differently across:

climates;
soils;
crops;
management histories;
intervention intensity;
duration.

A useful synthesis may therefore ask both:

What is the average effect?

and

How much do effects vary, and why?


Set up the later heterogeneity module. Avoid implying that heterogeneity is a statistical nuisance to be “fixed”. In applied ecology and agriculture it is often scientifically important.

What does a pooled effect mean?
Imagine the synthesis eventually estimates a response ratio of 1.10.
That could be described as:

On average, soil organic carbon was approximately 10% higher under reduced tillage than under the comparator in the included evidence.

But this statement alone tells us nothing about:

how variable effects are;
whether the estimate is dominated by particular studies;
risk of bias;
applicability to a new setting.


Stress the phrase “in the included evidence”. Avoid jumping from a pooled estimate to a universal causal statement. The strength of causal interpretation depends on study designs and assumptions.

Mini exercise: define the estimand
A review asks:

Do cover crops improve crop yield?

In pairs, specify:

population/context;
intervention;
comparator;
outcome;
effect measure;
target quantity.

Then ask: what decisions would change if the target were next-season yield versus mean yield over five years?

Allow about five minutes. There is deliberately no single perfect answer. Ask two groups to share their estimands and compare how small wording changes alter which studies/results would be eligible.

Learning outcomes
By the end, participants should be able to:

choose and interpret common effect-size measures;
calculate effect sizes and sampling variances;
fit and interpret random-effects meta-analysis;
distinguish uncertainty in the mean effect from heterogeneity among true effects;
use prediction intervals appropriately;
recognise and model dependent effect sizes;
use meta-regression cautiously;
conduct useful sensitivity analyses;
recognise limitations of publication-bias diagnostics; and
translate model results into appropriately qualified conclusions.

5. Triangulate
Imagine your search also found:

two conference abstracts with insufficient numerical data;
one trial registration listing soil carbon that was absent from the final paper;
several small studies conducted in different production systems from the large studies.

Write a short assessment of possible reporting bias that uses all of this information rather than relying on the funnel plot alone.

7. Reporting
Write a short results paragraph that includes:

the moderator being examined and why;
the estimated direction/magnitude and uncertainty;
residual heterogeneity;
a caution about across-study interpretation.

Do not report only the moderator p-value.

Part D — Decision-maker summary
Write four sentences summarising the synthesis for a decision-maker.
Requirements:

report the average effect;
acknowledge uncertainty;
describe between-study variation;
explain applicability cautiously;
do not use generic labels such as “low”, “moderate” or “high” heterogeneity.

5. Interpretation challenge
Write three sentences that separately communicate:

the average effect;
uncertainty in that average;
variation among effects / implications for a new comparable setting.

Avoid counting how many individual studies are statistically significant.

7. Decision challenge
Write 3–4 sentences explaining to a review team:

why treating every row as independent is questionable;
what the multilevel model improves;
what dependence assumption remains unresolved;
what you would seek or test in a full review.

2. Extract the quantities you need
From your preferred model, record:

summary(m)
predict(m, transf = exp)

Capture:

pooled effect on the analysis scale;
transformed relative effect;
95% confidence interval;
prediction interval;
heterogeneity estimate(s).

6. Decision-maker version
Write a maximum 80-word summary for a policy or management audience. It should include:

direction and approximate magnitude;
uncertainty and/or variation;
the most important qualification;
the population/settings to which the evidence most directly applies.

This page is intentionally a scaffold. Add the EU-NESA-recommended sources and current methodological references agreed with the Working Group before final release.
Suggested categories:

effect-size choice and interpretation;
random-effects meta-analysis and heterogeneity;
dependent effect sizes and multilevel models;
meta-regression;
reporting/publication bias;
transparent and reproducible reporting.

Learning objectives
By the end of this module, you should be able to:

explain why meta-analysis needs an effect size and sampling variance;
distinguish mean difference, standardised mean difference and response ratio;
select an effect measure that matches the synthesis question;
calculate log response ratios and sampling variances with metafor;
back-transform log response ratios for interpretation.


The goal is not to memorise every formula. Participants should understand what each measure means, what information it preserves or discards, and why the choice is substantive.

Precision is not study quality
Under a random-effects model:
\[w_i=\frac{1}{v_i+\tau^2}\]


Weight reflects

sampling precision;
estimated heterogeneity.


Weight does not guarantee

low risk of bias;
relevance;
good measurement;
causal identification.



A large, precise but biased study can receive substantial statistical weight. Precision and risk of bias are different dimensions. This distinction is worth repeating throughout the course.

Take-home

An effect size is part of the scientific question — not just an input to the model.

Before pooling, be able to explain:

the null value;
the direction of effect;
the real-world interpretation;
how uncertainty was calculated.


Transition: now that each study is expressed on a common scale with sampling uncertainty, we can ask how to synthesise the effects.

Learning objectives
By the end of this module, you should be able to:

explain heterogeneity in scientific rather than purely statistical terms;
distinguish \(\tau^2\), \(\tau\) and \(I^2\);
interpret a prediction interval;
explain why \(I^2\) is not a quality score;
identify plausible sources of heterogeneity;
recognise when an average effect is insufficient.


Start from the idea that observed estimates would differ even if the underlying effect were identical because of sampling variation. Heterogeneity is variation in underlying effects beyond that sampling variation.

Not all heterogeneity is ecological signal
Variation can arise from:

different outcome definitions;
measurement methods;
study designs;
analytical choices;
selective reporting;
risk of bias.


Explaining heterogeneity requires subject-matter and methodological judgement.


A meta-regression association with study design might indicate bias rather than a biological modifier. Statistical explanation is not automatically causal explanation.

Should we explain every bit of heterogeneity?
No. Meta-regression is most useful when moderators are:

scientifically motivated;
measured reasonably well;
represented across enough studies;
specified without excessive data dredging.


Residual heterogeneity is normal.


Foreshadow Module 5. Small meta-analyses contain little information for moderator effects. The goal is not to keep adding variables until tau-squared approaches zero.

A reporting checklist
A useful account of heterogeneous evidence may include:

pooled mean effect + confidence interval;
\(\tau^2\) or \(\tau\);
\(I^2\) as descriptive context;
prediction interval;
forest plot / distribution of effects;
plausible effect modifiers;
limitations in applicability and evidence quality.


The exact package depends on the evidence base. The principle is to communicate centre and spread, and to separate statistical variation from claims about why that variation occurs.

Learning objectives
By the end of this module, you should be able to:

distinguish subgroup analysis from meta-regression;
fit categorical and continuous moderators;
interpret coefficients on the lnRR and RR scales;
distinguish within-study causal effects from across-study associations;
recognise confounding, sparse data and multiplicity;
use moderators for explanation without overclaiming.

Start with a hypothesis, not a variable list
For our soil-carbon example we might hypothesise:

effects strengthen with intervention duration;
effects differ among climate categories;
baseline soil properties modify response;
management intensity changes the effect.


Specify the mechanism before inspecting the coefficient.


Ask participants which hypotheses are biologically plausible and which variables the teaching dataset actually contains. A moderator should earn its place scientifically, not because it happened to be recorded.

A familiar trap: across-study ≠ within-study
Imagine longer studies show larger effects.
Possible explanations include:

the effect truly accumulates with time;
longer studies occur in different climates;
different crops are studied for longer;
older/longer studies use different methods;
publication or follow-up processes differ.


Association can generate hypotheses without identifying the mechanism.


This is ecological confounding at the study level. If some primary studies themselves randomised or compared duration, those within-study contrasts would answer a different and potentially stronger causal question.

Report the pattern, not just the p-value
A useful report includes:

the prespecified moderator and rationale;
coefficient/effect estimate with uncertainty;
units and reference category;
number and distribution of studies;
residual heterogeneity;
limitations of across-study interpretation.


“Significant moderator” is not an interpretation.


Encourage a plot whenever possible. Readers need to see where the data lie and whether an apparent trend is driven by one or two studies.

Learning objectives
By the end of this module, you should be able to:

distinguish publication bias from broader selective reporting;
explain what funnel plots and small-study-effect tests can and cannot show;
recognise plausible alternative causes of funnel asymmetry;
use diagnostics cautiously in R;
combine statistical diagnostics with review-process information;
communicate uncertainty about missing evidence without claiming it has been “corrected”.

Why this matters for meta-analysis
If the probability that an estimate is observed depends on its result, then the observed evidence may not represent the evidence that was generated.
Possible consequences include:

biased pooled effects;
distorted heterogeneity;
misleading moderator relationships;
overconfidence in the apparent evidence base.


A larger meta-analysis is not necessarily a less biased meta-analysis.


Emphasise that increasing k does not solve systematic selection. Missing evidence is about the data-generating and reporting process, not merely sample size.

Asymmetry has many possible causes

selective publication or reporting;
genuine differences between small and large studies;
study quality or design differences;
different interventions, populations or settings;
effect-size/variance relationships;
chance, especially with few studies.


Funnel asymmetry is a clue to investigate, not a diagnosis.


This is the core message. Small studies may genuinely be different—for example, intensive experimental interventions at research stations versus larger pragmatic field trials.

Start earlier than the statistical diagnostics
Review methods can provide direct clues:

search grey literature and relevant repositories;
compare protocols/registrations with reports where available;
record missing outcomes and unusable results;
contact authors when important data are unavailable;
document conference abstracts and unpublished studies;
consider whether funding or dissemination pathways could affect availability.


Prevention and detection begin with the search and extraction process.


This makes the module part of evidence synthesis rather than a collection of post-hoc tests. Statistical diagnostics operate only on what was observed.
